Give labels an accessible value, so screen readers read gauges

A Geyser gauge is three labels, so a screen reader cannot tell its value, on the desktop or in a frontend that draws
labels itself: the value lives in Lua (Geyser.Gauge:setValue() sets self.value), and only the front label's width
shows it.

This adds setLabelAccessibleValue(name, value[, min, max]), getLabelAccessibleValue(name) and
clearLabelAccessibleValue(name). Their argument errors are the other label functions': nil and a message for an
empty or unknown name, an empty range or a number that is not finite, and a Lua error for an argument that is not a
number. A value past either end is taken as that end, as a progress bar shows it. TLabelModel keeps it and tells
the view of a real change through a new mAccessibleValueChanged callback, after a Role change when the label gains or
loses one.

- Desktop: while a label that is not a button has a value, TAccessibleLabel reports the progress bar role and
  implements QAccessibleValueInterface, TLabel sends a QAccessibleValueChangeEvent for a new value, and
  getLabelAccessibleRole() says "progressbar".
- Geyser.Gauge:setValue() sets it on the gauge's back label, out of the maximum it was given, or of 100, and
  setGauge() on a createGauge() gauge's, out of its maximum. A reading neither can give (not a number, or out of a
  maximum not above 0 or infinite) clears it, so a reader goes by what the gauge draws; they check this themselves
  rather than call setLabelAccessibleValue(), whose warning would come at every prompt. Each keeps the value and maximum it was given
  for laying the gauge out again (Geyser.Gauge:setStyleSheet(); moveGauge(), resizeGauge() and setGaugeWindow()),
  which until now passed the percentage or fraction and would have made it out of 100 or 1.
- Headless: THeadlessSession::LabelContent carries it as accessibleValue (value, min, max), so a change names the
  label in scriptWindowsChanged; listed among labelContent()'s fields in docs/headless.md.

Tested in UI_spec.lua, GeyserGauge_spec.lua and GUIUtils_spec.lua (the functions, their errors, both kinds of gauge),
HeadlessSessionWindowsTest (labelContent() and the change), LabelAccessibleInterfaceTest (the desktop interface and
its events) and ConsoleModelExtractionTest (refused with no view). Left for later: the gauge's text as the back
label's accessible description, which needs a description labels do not have yet.

For the wiki (Manual:Lua_Functions):
- setLabelAccessibleValue(labelName, value, [minimum], [maximum]): gives the label a value that a screen reader reads
  as a progress bar's, from minimum to maximum (0 and 100 when left out); a value past either end is that end.
  Returns true, or nil and a message. Geyser's and createGauge()'s gauges set it on their back label.
- getLabelAccessibleValue(labelName): the value, minimum and maximum, or nil for a label without one.
- clearLabelAccessibleValue(labelName): removes it, so the label is read as before. Returns true.

Carried by Mudlet/android's scripts/apply-mudlet-patches.sh until Mudlet/Mudlet has it.

